## 1. Goal

1. Orlando opens the castle, turns on stats and sees meaningful navigation FPS/frame intervals alongside clearly labeled CPU render time and draw calls.
2. Panning, held-key rotation, zooming and Walk feel responsive at the castle's current detail level. Hover and selection remain immediate.
3. Repeated entities and ordinary static geometry use substantially fewer draw calls, without changing the saved scene or requiring Orlando to redesign it for the renderer.
4. Editing a room, moving a hole, reshaping a ramp or changing an entity still previews correctly, saves one normal command and supports undo/redo.
5. The editor, saved shots and agent renders keep the same geometry and graybox appearance. Any optional quality tradeoff is explicit and local to the viewer.

## 2. Scope

### In

- Honest active-navigation frame pacing, CPU render timing, draw/triangle counters and repeatable browser profiling.
- Avoiding redundant pointer picking, broad React updates and repeated scene derivation during navigation, hover and stats updates.
- Instancing compatible ordinary instances, building on the existing array path.
- Batching static bodies, floors and edges with bounded rebuild cost and useful culling.
- Correct shadow invalidation and, where measurements justify it, an explicit viewer performance setting.
- Capture, Walk, selection, holes, isolation, entity editing and terrain-rendering regression checks.

### Out (deliberately deferred)

- Terrain-aware walking, placement and gameplay queries from phase 16 §10.
- Changes to authored geometry, scene schemas, scene history, library history or Unity export structure to suit batching.
- Server/network/persistence optimization unless profiling identifies a direct navigation dependency; record unrelated findings for later.
- WebGPU, a new renderer, occlusion culling, automatic geometry simplification, mesh LOD, and a general worker/job framework.
- New lighting/art direction, mandatory lower image quality, and permanent telemetry or a performance dashboard.

## 3. Measurement contract

The existing view renders on demand. An idle gap is not a slow frame, and the first frame after idle must not poison navigation statistics. Stats must not create a continuous render loop merely to keep themselves fresh.

| Metric | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Active frame interval / FPS | Time between rendered frames during sustained navigation; FPS is derived from elapsed time and frame count over that window. Exclude idle, background-tab, pause and capture intervals. |
| CPU render time | Synchronous duration of the renderer call. Explicitly labeled; does not include all JavaScript work or GPU completion. |
| Slow frames | Navigation frame intervals above the recorded refresh/frame budget; retain median and p95 intervals in benchmark results so averages do not hide hitches. |
| Draw calls / triangles | Renderer counters for the editor frame, with shadow inclusion documented and capture passes kept separate. |
| Shapes | Expanded shapes as today; not a count of GPU draw calls. |

Browser traces should attribute pointer handlers, React commits, derived scene work, render submission and Walk surface rays. Record GPU evidence when the available browser supports it; unavailable GPU timing is not zero GPU cost. Prefer a temporary/local profiling harness to permanent detailed instrumentation.

For every comparison record commit, browser/OS/hardware, development or production build, canvas CSS size, device pixel ratio, quality settings, camera/route, scene counts and whether stats are enabled. Use the same conditions before and after. Warm up first, repeat each sustained navigation scenario at least three times, and distinguish load/first-pick/first-edit hitches from steady-state costs. Compare stats on and off to check instrumentation overhead.

## 4. Renderer constraints

- The server-owned scene remains the source of truth. Batches, matrices, buffers and lookup tables are disposable derived state.
- Cache invalidation follows actual inputs: geometry, cutters, entity definitions, transforms, visibility/isolation and material/highlight state. Cursor, stats and camera reports must not invalidate unrelated geometry.
- Reuse compatible entity geometry; a part cut by an external hole falls back to its correct variant. Preserve entity pivots, scale, pitch, roll, inherited visibility and missing-entity behavior.
- Keep picking IDs and group/instance/array ownership unchanged. Editor data picking and Walk's rendered-mesh surface queries must both work after batching, including transformed instances and floor holes.
- Batch by compatible material and render behavior. Preserve body/floor shading, tiled UVs, creased normals, edge styling, transparency and shadow flags. Hole ghosts, notes, lines, gizmos and live previews may retain separate paths.
- Avoid one scene-wide batch that destroys useful frustum culling. Compare spatial chunks or a supported batching approach against merged buffers; select using measured frame time, rebuild cost and memory. Do not choose an API solely because it reduces the counter.
- Hover/selection must highlight exactly the intended shapes without rebuilding the whole scene on each pointer move. Temporary overlays or exclusion from static batches are implementation choices to benchmark.
- Dispose replaced buffers/materials and cancel stale derived work. Scene switches, entity-definition edits, undo and repeated select/deselect cycles must not accumulate GPU resources.
- Captures use canonical geometry and their requested resolution. Existing terrain drag previews and full-resolution capture/export behavior remain intact. A renderer refactor must not accidentally reevaluate terrain on each cursor update.

## 5. Fixtures and benchmark scenarios

Create fixtures only on a scratch server (`PORT=5171 DATA_DIR=<scratch>/data npm run dev`, or another free port). Use MCP/editor operations, not direct scene-file access. Keep reproducible setup and measurement instructions with the benchmark results.

| Fixture | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Castle | Read-only profiling of Orlando's open scene when browser access is available, or a copy created through supported scene operations. Record which was used. Never mutate the original for benchmarks. |
| Repetition | 400 ordinary instances of a single-box entity, then equivalent arrays; compare incremental calls above an empty-scene baseline. Include a multipart entity and scaled/tilted placements. |
| Mixed geometry | Hundreds of plain shapes, rooms, cylinders, curved/concave free-forms and ramps; holes crossing ordinary and entity geometry; several colors. |
| Terrain | Generated terrain with modifiers plus ordinary buildings; verify cached navigation, source editing, Show modifiers, drag preview and canonical captures. Reuse phase 16's practical limits. |

For each applicable fixture measure: steady pan, held-key rotation, sustained zoom, select hover across shapes, Walk movement/look, first pick after an edit, edit preview/release and undo. Use a fixed route and camera for before/after comparisons. Include a small scene to detect overhead that only helps large scenes.

## 6. Acceptance and performance targets

- **Correctness is required:** the browser checklist (§8) passes and screenshots show no unintended geometry, material or shadow changes.
- **Smoothness target:** on Orlando's recorded reference setup, target sustained 60 FPS where the display/browser supports it (16.7 ms frame budget), with p95 active intervals within 1.5 refresh intervals on the benchmark routes. Record actual refresh cadence; do not promise this across arbitrary hardware or terrain resolutions.
- **Baseline gate:** 17.1 records actual current results and identifies the dominant costs. If the target is infeasible on the reference hardware, record evidence and bring a concrete quality/scope tradeoff to Orlando before changing the target.
- **Ordinary-instance target:** the 400-single-box fixture adds at most 4 render calls above baseline in the default view, including its shadow pass. Multipart entities scale with compatible parts/materials, not placement count. Document any renderer-specific extra pass.
- **Static batching target:** at least 50% fewer calls on the fixed mixed fixture, while improving navigation frame time and avoiding worse p95 edit-release hitches. Record castle results separately; do not apply the screenshot's 767 calls to a different camera as a baseline.
- **No hidden regressions:** compare small-scene navigation, edit latency, memory/resource counts and capture time. Investigate repeatable regressions above run-to-run noise before accepting an increment.
- An optimization is accepted for its measured effect and correctness. If a proposed technique does not help, record the experiment and decision; do not silently mark an unimplemented increment complete.

## 7. Implementation increments

### 17.1 — Measure navigation honestly

- Correct the stats labels; add active-navigation FPS/frame intervals with explicit idle/background reset behavior and bounded UI update frequency.
- Build the reproducible fixture/profile workflow and baseline results in `plans/17-performance-measurements.md`.
- Compare pan, rotation, zoom, hover and Walk; separate development overhead from a production build. Attribute the leading costs and test shadow/DPR sensitivity using temporary diagnostic controls.
- **Checks:** focused timing tests for idle, resume, window aggregation and capture exclusion; full checks from the preamble; browser stats-on/off comparison and an idle-demand-render check.
- **Exit:** a trustworthy baseline and written implementation priorities. Continue with 17.2; evidence can change the order of 17.3–17.5 if the decision is recorded here.

### 17.2 — Keep navigation and hover cheap

- Dispatch navigation before work that navigation does not need. Reuse picking results and coalesce high-rate pointer work to frames where appropriate; preserve exact click/drawing behavior and final pointer state.
- Contain cursor/stats updates and stabilize derived scene/instancing inputs. Recompute expansions, cutter relationships and relevant geometry only when their inputs change.
- Profile React commits, picking and first-pick costs. Add spatial indexing or lazy hit-mesh work only if the trace justifies it.
- **Checks:** pan/Space-hand, hover, cursor, drawing on surfaces, snapping, marquee, gizmos and undo; locked/hidden/isolated behavior; no stale cursor or selection after edits. Repeat baseline scenarios and capture checks.
- **Exit:** lower measured interaction overhead with unchanged editing behavior; record any remaining bottleneck.

### 17.3 — Instance ordinary repeated entities

- Share the compatible instancing path between ordinary instances and arrays while keeping stable ownership for highlights, picking and Walk.
- Group compatible parts/materials, maintain transformed bounds and preserve fallback variants for external cuts, incomplete visibility and missing definitions.
- Ensure scene and entity-definition changes invalidate the correct batches; keep capture rendering in parity.
- **Checks:** repetition fixture meets §6; multipart/scaled/tilted entities, external holes, selected/hovered instances, arrays, Edit entity mode, definition edits and undo; Walk floor queries through instances and holes; captures and resource disposal.
- **Exit:** ordinary repetition scales by compatible entity parts rather than placement count, with recorded before/after results.

### 17.4 — Batch ordinary static geometry

- Compare merged spatial batches with the installed renderer's supported batching options. Record the choice before expanding the implementation.
- Batch eligible static bodies/floors/edges; retain correct live preview and highlight paths. Rebuild affected batches rather than everything on pointer movement.
- Preserve culling, holes, terrain coexistence and clean capture behavior; measure initial builds and edit-release cost as well as navigation.
- **Checks:** mixed fixture meets §6; pan through near/far views, selection crossing batches, multiselect/group highlights, room-floor cuts, free-form/ramp/tilt geometry, visibility/isolation, copy/mirror/scale, undo, scene switching, Walk and capture parity.
- **Exit:** measured draw and frame-time reduction with bounded rebuild cost and no resource growth after repeated edits.

### 17.5 — Shadows, quality and final acceptance

- Cache shadows only when caster geometry and light/shadow-camera state permit. Invalidate for edits, previews, transforms, visibility, scene changes and navigation that changes light coverage. Verify a fresh shadow map before captures.
- If 17.1 or subsequent measurements show a pixel/GPU bottleneck, add a small, explicit local viewer quality control for resolution/shadows, using existing UI conventions. Preserve the current appearance by default and canonical capture resolution. Omit the control if it has no useful measured effect, recording why.
- Repeat the complete benchmark matrix and provide the concrete reference setup, before/after values, limitations and Orlando's acceptance checklist.
- **Checks:** no frozen/missing shadows on pan, zoom, Walk, edit, undo or capture; high-DPR comparison; default visual parity; production build, full tests/typecheck and §8.
- **Exit:** automated/browser verification complete and Orlando's hands-on smoothness/visual acceptance recorded separately. Any unresolved target remains explicitly open.

## 8. Browser checklist

1. Navigate the castle using pan, zoom, held-key rotation and Walk; record frame pacing and how movement feels. Pause and resume without a misleading FPS dip.
2. Hover/select ordinary shapes, instances, arrays and groups; marquee and multiselect; enter/leave isolation and Edit entity mode. Check locks, hidden nodes and hole ghosts.
3. Move/resize/tilt a room or volume, move a cutting hole, edit a curved free-form/ramp and copy repeated entities. Check preview, release, undo and redo without flicker or stale batches.
4. Walk over rooms, ramps, scaled/tilted entities and externally cut floors. Existing terrain-walking limitations remain separately documented.
5. Edit terrain modifiers and toggle Show modifiers; inspect drag previews and canonical release results without repeated evaluation on navigation.
6. Compare fixed-camera editor screenshots, saved shots and agent plan/sheet/node/eye renders where supported. Compare geometry, outlines, materials, clipping and shadows; distinguish expected capture presentation from regressions.
7. Switch scenes and edit definitions repeatedly; inspect resource counts after cleanup. Confirm the renderer becomes idle after navigation and updates immediately after a remote edit.

## 9. Decisions

1. Measure the full navigation experience before selecting expensive renderer work. The screenshot alone establishes neither low FPS nor its cause.
2. Keep optimizations derived and viewer-side, preserving authored scenes and shared undo behavior.
3. Build on array instancing and shared baking; retain correct individual variants where batching is incompatible.
4. Keep default visual quality. Explicit viewer settings are available only if a measured tradeoff warrants them.
5. This phase does not close phase 16's outstanding Unity visual acceptance or implement terrain-aware gameplay queries.

